WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.620
 Hello, and welcome to Conversations with Computing.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:07.160
 In this column, I take a look at some of the second-order effects of the technology that

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.920
 Steve Jobs produced throughout his career and how those technologies from Apple and

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.000
 Next often served as an inspiration to many of the early innovators in the Internet and

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.120
 World Wide Web.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.600
 I found out that we'd lost Steve Jobs on the evening of October 5th, right in the middle

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.760
 of a lecture on using regular expressions in Python.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.600
 I was recording the lecture for a later podcast.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.400
 Because you might want to go down the nerd rabbit hole later, and that's okay.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.000
 There's nothing wrong with avoiding the nerd rabbit hole, but this is a nerdy thing.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.920
 It's extremely nerdy.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.760
 I think it's awesome.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.800
 A nerdy announcement?

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.800
 >> Well, yeah.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.960
 >> Really, I mean, and it's kind of verified it's not some...

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:05.660
 >> The very next week, in the same classroom, I was giving my lecture on the inside story

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.080
 of the history of the Internet and the World Wide Web, sharing some of my interviews with

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.940
 early pioneers.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.700
 As I gave the lecture and watched my video interviews thinking about Steve Jobs, I began

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:22.520
 to realize how important Apple and Next technology was to those early innovators.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.160
 In some ways, the very existence of those technologies helped propel the Internet and

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.560
 Web revolution forward.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.480
 In my 1999 interview with Robert Caillou, the co-inventor of the World Wide Web, we

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.460
 are sitting in his office by the NextCube that ran the very first web server.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.420
 As Robert describes how the NextStep development environment allowed them to quickly build

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:49.800
 prototype versions of a web browser in 1990, you get the sense that their Next hardware

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.720
 and software was very much an equal partner in their early visions of the Web.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.120
 >> Obviously, the whole of high-energy physics has been this sort of miniature information

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.080
 society since way back when, as soon as there were networks, essentially.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:11.880
 And so because we have this need for spreading documentation around, we built these things

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:17.560
 like centralized databases, there was CERN doc, you know, you could use it, but whatever.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:26.520
 We had a--well, we still have a large database of high-energy physics articles kept by Stanford,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.700
 and you could get at it before the Web by knowing exactly what computer to log into

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.840
 over the network, blah, blah, blah, blah, but it was all very difficult.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.640
 And then so when Tim invented the Web, and I have a separate proposal, I dropped it because

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:45.560
 Tim's proposal ran over the Internet, and that was clearly much more efficient.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.840
 When the Web came, all that necessity of knowing which computer to go to, what to say to that

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.960
 computer, and so forth, just disappeared, you know.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.320
 People put up these pages with their links, and you could just follow links and get to

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.240
 places where you wanted to be and find everything, and it was also all in the same format.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.880
 So that was very important, too, that, you know, we broke this proprietary commercial

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:13.280
 system of vertical markets, which, you know, don't let you get at anything, except if you

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.520
 stay with this particular company or with that particular company.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:23.720
 So that horizontal split, that cut that we made between the browsers on top and the databases

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:29.020
 at the bottom was, I think, essential to make it useful for us, but also to make it useful

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.440
 for everybody else, right?

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:39.500
 And so that was what it was like in the beginning, and Tim and I did this all on this next machine

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:43.280
 here in about 1990.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:52.320
 So the first server was about 1990, the first, end of 1990, the first server in the United

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.280
 States came up about a year later at Stanford because of that database that I was talking

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.960
 about before.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:07.800
 The real problem was that this development system is so much better than anything else

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:15.000
 that porting what we had here to any other platform took an order of magnitude more time.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.100
 And for example, every time you clicked here, you had another window, right?

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.800
 Every time you clicked on a diagram, you had a diagram in another window.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.120
 When you clicked on a map, you got the map in PostScript, scalable, perfectly printable,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.120
 and so on and so forth.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.840
 You tried to port that to another system, you got berserk.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.400
 You know, there's a big difference between making an editor and something that just puts

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.560
 up a page and you can't do anything with that.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.000
 So our system from 1990 was also the editor.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.000
 I mean, I started, it's only after Next stopped making hardware and I had to go back from

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.720
 a Next to a Macintosh that I had to learn HTML, right?

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.920
 I mean, before we produced all the documentation and the stuff, but we never saw any, we never

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.080
 saw any HTML, we never saw any URLs, right?

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:13.600
 Because you linked by saying link this to that, not by typing in the URL.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.160
 There was a special window you could call up in which you could type a URL if you needed

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.200
 to, but it wasn't the usual thing.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.720
 I mean, there's this navigation panel which says HTTP, dah, dah, dah, dah, and I learned

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:24.960
 all that.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.760
 The hard way afterwards that you had to use that because you've lost that system, right?

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:36.080
 Steve Jobs' Next Workstations were essential to the creation of the earliest web software.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.400
 Their advanced development environment and rich display capabilities led early innovators

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:47.480
 to think of the internet less as a text only and more as an engaging multimedia experience.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.800
 In my 2007 interview with Paul Coons, who brought up the first web server in the United

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:58.040
 States on an IBM mainframe at the Stanford Linear Accelerator in December 1991, we can

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:03.700
 see that he still has a working color Next Workstation in his office.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.520
 When Paul was developing software for the IBM mainframe, there were around 20 web servers

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.840
 in the entire world.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:16.280
 If you wanted a web browser to test your server, having a Next Workstation was essential.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:22.540
 So when I was at CERN in September 1991, and Tim DeMarenz Lee dragged me into his office

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:30.400
 to give me a demo of the web, at first I wasn't very interested, but when he demonstrated

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:37.280
 doing a query to a help system database on a mainframe, I immediately put two and two

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:41.320
 together and said, "Well, if you can query a help system on a mainframe, you can query

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.720
 a database on a mainframe."

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.720
 The database itself had about 300,000 entries.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:52.200
 Was it heavily used before you put it on the web and then continued to be heavily used?

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:53.480
 It was heavily used.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:07:02.680
 People had signed up for have computer accounts on the mainframe so they could do their queries

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:03.680
 from all around the world.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.640
 I think there were some 4,000 registered users.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.240
 So did you have to write it all from scratch?

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.440
 Did you write it from the protocol or was there software that you reused to make your

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:13.440
 first web server?

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.480
 Well, I used the CERN server software, which was written in C. Unfortunately, we had a

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.560
 C compiler on the mainframe at the time.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.120
 It wasn't very long that we had a C compiler, but we had one.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:35.600
 So all I had to do was to write some extra C code to get the query that the user had

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.720
 made and turn it into the database query.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:46.440
 Now, wasn't the original web server on a Next?

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:50.080
 All the web software was originally developed on the Next computer at CERN.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.200
 While the web and web technologies were gaining traction in the academic and research communities

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:59.480
 who often had access to powerful Unix workstations on their desks, the rest of the world was

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:03.960
 browsing the Internet using the text-oriented gopher clients and servers.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:09.120
 In 1993, the National Center for Supercomputing Applications at the University of Illinois

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.080
 at Urbana-Champaign released NCSA Mosaic.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:19.000
 Mosaic was the first graphically rich web browser that ran on Unix, Macintosh, and Windows.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:24.400
 In my 1997 interview with Larry Smarr, he points out that the success of the NCSA Mosaic

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.020
 browser occurred in part because of earlier work developing the NCSA image library to

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:35.060
 take advantage of the graphics capabilities of early Apple Macintosh computers.

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:43.520
 What we basically did during that late age period was to make the world safe for images.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:50.480
 What NCSA image did was basically we said we want to build a world of infrastructure

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:58.640
 in which it's as easy to move an image around as it is to move a word.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:04.260
 That was our design parameters, and that's the way we talked about it back then.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:09.280
 So that meant we had to scale the network, scale the disk drives, scale the compute power.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:11.360
 We had to go to full color.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.960
 When the Mac II first came out, 256 color levels, we got 50 of them.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.120
 Apple gave us 50 Mac IIs, which was stunning in those days.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:27.200
 We were, in fact, the largest funded academic group in the country for the Apple Advanced

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:28.920
 Technology Group.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:33.920
 IBM at that time was telling their customers, "You don't need color.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.200
 We've already provided it, as I said.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.560
 You have four of them, black, white, cyan, and magenta.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.480
 Why would you need more?"

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:48.540
 So what we did was we took things that were on $100,000 computer graphics workstations

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:53.800
 of image processing that medical imaging people use, satellite reconnaissance people use,

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:59.280
 and we took all that, put it in the software, and NCSA image on the Mac.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:03.040
 So you could just move the mouse and do what it would take you, what would have otherwise

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:06.960
 cost you $100,000 to do, and you'd have to be an elite specialist.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.560
 But again, taking things that elite people knew how to do, could afford to do, and making

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:13.480
 it available to the masses.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:17.120
 The day after I found out that Steve was no longer with us, I happened to have a morning

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.600
 meeting in New York City, so I went to the Apple Store in Manhattan to get a sense of

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.360
 our collective reaction to the loss of such a brilliant visionary who's affected us in

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:27.360
 so many ways.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.360
 Hello, everybody.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.360
 This is Chuck.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.360
 I'm here at the Apple Store in Manhattan.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.600
 I'll show you what we're seeing.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:42.540
 We're seeing press, we're seeing an impromptu memorial for Steve Jobs that's got apples,

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:48.760
 it's got flowers, it's got all kinds of really, really cool stuff.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.840
 But the crowd seems really quiet.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.040
 There's a media that's interviewing all the people.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.560
 You can see the media there grabbing people off the street and interviewing them.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.700
 I'm sure they're capturing their thoughts.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:08.000
 So here we are sort of one day after the passing of Steve Jobs.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.760
 Throughout his career at Apple and next, and then again at Apple, Steve Jobs was never

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:17.700
 interested in the least expensive nor the most profitable technology.

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:20.900
 He pursued the best and most advanced technology.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:26.160
 In many of his designs and decisions, he provided a roadmap for the entire consumer technology

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.520
 marketplace.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:32.320
 It's no wonder that many of us would wait with breathless anticipation for each and

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.420
 every Apple announcement.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:38.560
 They were always an exciting glimpse into the future of technology.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.320
 Over the past 30 years, the products that came from jobs companies were a joy to use.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:48.240
 And perhaps more importantly, they inspired many innovators to keep their focus on the

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.080
 exciting and unexplored future.

